===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}

{{liushu|p}} – a horse with its head facing the left, showing a flowing mane in the wind, standing vertically on two legs.<ref>{{cite-web |en |url=https://languagelog.ldc.upenn.edu/nll/?p=72810 |title=Evolution of the sinograph and the word for horse |first=Victor |last=Mair |work={{w|Language Log}} |date=2026-02-16 |accessdate=2026-02-16 |publisher={{w|University of Pennsylvania}}}}</ref> In the bronze inscriptions, the head was often simplified into an eye ({{m|zh|目}}). The legs eventually evolved into four dots ({{m|zh|灬}}, unrelated to {{m|zh|火}}).

Contrast with {{m|zh|鹿|t=deer}}, which saw a very different development, and {{m|zh|𢊁}} (as in {{m|zh|薦}}), which is a hybrid: it has the legs of {{lang|zh|馬}} ({{m|zh|灬}}) but the head of {{m|zh|鹿}}.

===Etymology 1===
{{zh-forms|s=马|alt=𫠉,傌,𢒗,𢒠,𢒧,𩡬,𩡮}}
{{wp|zh:,yue:,gan:,hak:mâ,cdo:mā,nan:bé,wuu:马}}

[[Wanderwort]] ({{zh-ref|Jacques, 2009}}; {{zh-ref|Felner and Hill, 2019}}; {{zh-ref|Lai et al., 2020}}). This word is often compared to other {{cog|sit}} forms, e.g., {{cog|bo|རྨང}}, {{cog|my|မြင်း}}, {{cog|sit-jap|mbro}}, {{cog|txg|𘆝}}, {{cog|cdm|tr=sĕraŋ}}, {{cog|tbq-kuk-pro|*raŋ}} (> {{cog|cnh|ràŋ}}) ({{zh-ref|Benedict, 1972}}; {{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}; STEDT), but the lack of nasal coda in Chinese would be irregular, suggesting borrowing from a sister language rather than common ancestry ({{zh-ref|Sagart et al., 2019}}). Outside of {{cog|sit}}, this has also been compared to {{cog|mn|ᠮᠣᠷᠢ}}, {{cog|ko|말}}, {{cog|ja|-}} {{ja-r|馬|うま}} ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}; {{zh-ref|Lai et al., 2020}}).

The sense of “big” is derived from “horse”; compare the English uses of {{m|en|horse}}. For the insect prefix sense, see {{m|zh|螞}}. It has converged with the sense of “big”.

The surname is popularly known to be prevalent among [[Hui]] Muslims, where it is likely derived from {{der|zh|ar|مُحَمَّد|t=Muhammad}}, although the surname {{lang|zh|馬}} itself predates Islam.

